A||N||3||MikeyJP||Paint||||1||
Z||000000||MikeyJP||12-31-2001||11:19 AM||||Everything on my '86 SLE is cherry except the red paint or gel coat which is fading and starting to go white in spots. I tired everything over the counter but obviously the finish is just cooked. Spoke to four different body shops over the past year about re painting and got four totally different answers. One swears he can put decent enough paint right over it (with some prep work) for about $600.   The last swears it is a complicated technical process that to do right will cost about $6,000. and anything else will peal, crack and alligator within two years.   Then I heard from two others in the middle who thought they could do some sort of expoxy gel for about $1500-1800. Whats the real story? <BR>Mike P.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark Matthews||12-31-2001||12:14 PM||||There was a thread about two weeks ago about this.  I had the same problem.  I can't reember who recommended it, but if you use a couple nice sharp razor blades you can peel off the clear coat.<P>After I peeled it off I wet sanded it with 400 grit sand paper.  The car looks about 20 times better.  And is a great free temp fix until I decde what I'm doing with the car.<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bill Shenefelt||12-31-2001||12:15 PM||||I think there are two different things you could try. First, it may be the clear coat only that is bad.  If you wet sand it off and reclear coat that should work.  If you want to do the whole job, I would wet sand down to primer or as a inimum till you see only red coming off and then paint with clearcoat.  If you want to use laquor, you need to seal the old paint, but sand well first so it does not get too thick.  I will email you with a post I did a couple of days ago as to how I did my 86.<P>------------------<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||12-31-2001||01:42 PM||||a decent repaint with rite prep should be in the $1500-$2500 range. You do have to get all the bad clear (white) off. You can sand it or like they said, use a single edge razor blade, then sand whats left. You dont have to sand to primer or even thru basecoat if its not surface cracked. It can be sprayed rite over. You CANNOT just spray a new coat of clear over the old basecoat. It has to be applied during a certain period after the base color. It wont last, even though it might look ok. Ive done this since the 70's and in my own body shop. Prep is 75% of the job. If your gonna strip off the bad clear, why not spend the little extra effort and do it right. You can save a ton too, If you do all the stripping and sanding yourself, then take it to painter. They wont warranty your work, but you know if you got it right so who cares. Doing it that way will save you over 1/2.||||reg||1
